```

> I would prefer to commit this as is (per sympy.this "commit now", principle).

To be clear that principle holds no weight and it is in fact an extremely bad idea to have a principle like that in a large software project that is used by many people downstream. We absolutely should think carefully about things before making changes because we always need to be careful about backwards compatibility. There are two sides to backwards compatibility:
1. Trying to avoid making breaking changes now
2. Trying not to create situations where breaking changes would be needed in future

Almost all discussions about "backwards compatibility" in SymPy focus on the first of these rules but the second is actually far more important. When introducing a new public method on Basic or when changing an existing method we need to be very careful to try and ensure that we will never want to change the exact semantics of that method in future. Following this principle all methods should be conservative and the possible set of valid inputs should be clearly defined.

The implementation of `has/has_free` currently in this PR is sufficiently complex (the cryptic variable names don't help) that I am not even sure what its exact semantics are. Below I show a simpler version but that fails some tests: that shows to me that the version in the PR is semantically different so that it is clear to me that it has behaviour that I would not expect even if it isn't clear exactly how they differ.

I would prefer something simpler and clearer like this:
```python
    def _has(self, iterargs, patterns):
        patterns_instance = set()
        patterns_class = []
        patterns_matcher = []
        for p in patterns:
            if isinstance(p, type):
                assert issubclass(p, Basic)
                patterns_class.append(p)
            else:
                p = _sympify(p)
                get_matcher = getattr(p, '_has_matcher', None)
                if get_matcher is not None:
                    patterns_matcher.append(get_matcher())
                else:
                    patterns_instance.add(p)

        if patterns_instance:
            if any(arg in patterns_instance for arg in iterargs(self)):
                return True

        if patterns_class:
            types = tuple(patterns_class)
            types_found = set(map(type, iterargs(self)))
            if any(issubclass(t, types) for t in types_found):
                return True

        if patterns_matcher:
            # This is the slow path (to be avoided where possible):
            for arg in iterargs(self):
                for matcher in patterns_matcher:
                    if matcher(arg):
                        return True

        return False

# Also delete the Basic _has_matcher method
```
I can see that this version fails a couple of tests. This test should be changed:
https://github.com/sympy/sympy/blob/ed0bfd1e47345c8bd45ef542eef18f6a21c55685/sympy/core/tests/test_expr.py#L1129-L1131
Since `f` and `g` are not `Basic` instances `Tuple(f, g)` should really raise an error. In any case it should not be necessary for `has/has_free` to support broken Basic instances like this.

The other test that fails is this one:
https://github.com/sympy/sympy/blob/ed0bfd1e47345c8bd45ef542eef18f6a21c55685/sympy/core/tests/test_power.py#L257-L258
The problem is something to do with `exp(x)` as a function vs `E**x` as a power. That possibly needs some special case handling via `_has_matcher` in `Pow` and/or `exp` but ideally we should avoid `_has_matcher` where possible as it takes us back down the slow path. Probably there is a bug somewhere that causes us to have a `Pow` when we should really have `exp` and that bug should be fixed rather than using `_has_matcher`.

