For testing purposes it is usually better to have a simple `__eq__` method that checks for exact structural equality rather than mathematical equality so that the test asserts exactly what the object is. The method here would also allow a `ModuleElement` to compare equal to a `PowerBasis` or an `int`, `ZZ` or `QQ` instance. Sometimes that kind of thing is useful but sometimes it just blurs type-safety.

At a basic level this happens a lot in Python e.g. `1 == 1.0` but that leads to other problems e.g.:
```python
>>> {1, 1.0}
{1}
>>> {1.0, 1}
{1.0}
```
For the most part I tend to think that you should only have objects compare equal with `==` if you would be happy for one object to be exchanged with the other. Here I would not want to exchange a float value with an int so I do not want them to compare equal.

